{"name":"Serape","hex":"#d88b4d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"serape","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of blanket worn as a cloak, especially by Spanish-Americans, or used as a saddle blanket."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/serape"}],"entries":[{"title":"Serape","blocks":[{"text":"The serape, sarape or jorongo is a long blanket-like shawl or cloak, often brightly colored and fringed at the ends, worn in Mexico, especially by men. The accepted spelling of the word in Mexico and Spanish-speaking countries is sarape. The term serape is for the rectangular woven blanket, though in more recent years it can also be used to refer to a very soft rectangular blanket with an opening in the middle for one's head, similar to a poncho, called gabán, or jorongo in Mexico. Modern variations of some serapes are made with matching hoods for head covering. The length varies, but front and back normally reach knee length on an average person."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Sarape_1850.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serape"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Serape_MET_hzTR_205_1_2002_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Serape_MET_hzTR_205_1_2002_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Serape_MET_hzTR_205_1_2002_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Serape_MET_hzTR_205_1_2002_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Classic Saltillo Serape, circa 1825","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASerape_MET_hzTR_205_1_2002_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Mexican_Serape.jpg/330px-Mexican_Serape.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Mexican_Serape.jpg/330px-Mexican_Serape.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Traditional serapes are worn like a shawl or cloak. Its alteration into a poncho-like clothing item is more recent.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMexican_Serape.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Sarape_mexicano._%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Sarape_mexicano._%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Sarape_mexicano._%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Sarape_mexicano._%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Sarape_mexicano._%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Sarape_mexicano._%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Modern mexican sarape.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASarape_mexicano._(cropped).jpg"}],"sounds":{"serape":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-serape.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/2b/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-serape.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-serape.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/2b/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-serape.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Flame%2C_not_lame-serape.wav.ogg"}]}}}